 of  guy whereas this will contain two what  you can do is by comparing our unknown  sample with the other samples we can see  our unknown sample matches up with B  meaning our unknown sample is coloring  dye B so that's how we can use  chromatography to and to discover what  is in an unknown sample now what we can  also do is this near the top of the  chromatography paper we have something  called a solvent front so all that is is  we can see that's just how far the  solvent has soaked through and what we  can do is we can use this solvent front  to find something called the RF value  now all that RF value is is it's a  particular number which corresponds to  to a particular dye it's a little bit  like a fingerprint so by finding this  value will be able to say that for  example if a will have a particular  value it will always have that  particular RF value I'll show you how to  calculate an RF value so what you need  is you need first of all a ruler now the  calculation that we're going to be let's  say we're finding the RF value of this  orange dot here we need to do the  distance traveled by the solvent so I  traveled by the substance by the  substance divided by the distance  travelled by the solvent so I'll show  you how to do that now what we do in is  to the middle point of the dot we are  measuring from the line to the center  part of the dot so that's for this  particular one is three centimeters  three point zero centimeters  I then divide it by the distance moved  by the solvent so where the sole from  this line up to the soles in front so  that one there this is the distance I'm  measuring with my ruler and that is  eight point five centimeters moved by  the solvent so all I need to do next to  find my RF value is ID 3 divided by  eight point five which gives me this  number here I'm going to round it to two  decimal places so my RF value is 0.35  now this value is just a little bit like  I said like a fingerprint so that guy  will always have that RF 